# Insert `Ctrl-A` types the text the last Insert typed (`:help i_CTRL-A`;
# nvchad-probe: `Afoo<Esc>o<C-a><Esc>` on `x` -> `xfoo` / `foo`).
# `Ctrl-R =` would read the expression register, which mnml does not
# have: the line says so and the expression typed after it is dropped up
# to its Enter, instead of landing in the buffer (it used to type
# `2*21` and break the line on the Enter).
write f.txt "x\n"
open f.txt
command editor.use_vim
key A
type foo
key esc
key o
key ctrl+a
key esc
ex w
expect file f.txt contains "xfoo\nfoo\n"
key A
type " "
key ctrl+r
type =
expect screen contains "expression register not supported"
type 2*21
key enter
type Z
key esc
ex w
expect file f.txt contains "xfoo\nfoo Z\n"
expect file f.txt lacks "2*21"
